# managed-by: nightingale-installer
#
# Nightingale LAN front-door for self-hosted web mode.
#
# Both HTTP and HTTPS proxy to the same plain-HTTP nightingale.service on
# 127.0.0.1:8080. HTTP is the default reachable URL: pages load on every
# device with no per-device setup, but browsers will tag the origin
# "Not Secure" because `*.local` is NOT a secure context per the W3C
# Secure Contexts spec (only `localhost` / `*.localhost` / loopback IPs /
# `https://` / `wss://` / `file://` are). That label is harmless for
# normal pages, but it does block the secure-context-only browser APIs
# the app uses for mic capture (`navigator.mediaDevices.getUserMedia`),
# clipboard, fullscreen, etc.
#
# To enable those, the user installs the trust root we publish at
# /root.crt and visits `https://<host>.local` instead - Caddy mints
# certs from its own local CA on first contact via the `:443` block
# below, so every SNI hostname/IP gets a valid cert without any
# per-host config.
#
# Because HTTPS is opt-in (and the trust-install dance is per device),
# we deliberately do NOT redirect HTTP -> HTTPS. A redirect would force
# every visitor through the cert-install flow before they can even see
# the app, which is the opposite of what we want.
#
# Intentionally no global options block (`{ ... }` at the top) so this
# file can be imported into an operator's existing Caddyfile via
# `import Caddyfile.d/*.caddy` from any position - global options must be
# the first server block in the effective config, which can't be
# guaranteed when we're added as an import. The `:443` block uses the
# per-site `tls internal { on_demand }` shorthand instead of the global
# `local_certs` directive; both end up using Caddy's local CA, and the
# per-site form also waives the `ask` endpoint requirement that on-demand
# TLS otherwise imposes (internal CA can't issue malicious certs).

# ── HTTP listener (default working path) ────────────────────────────────
#
# Matches every hostname/IP on port 80 - `<host>.local`, the bare LAN IP,
# and any other name resolving here all work without extra config.
#
# Two handlers, in priority order:
#   1. /root.crt is served straight from /etc/caddy so devices that *do*
#      want HTTPS can bootstrap trust over plain HTTP. (HTTPS would
#      chicken-and-egg the cert install.) The cert lives in the same
#      directory as this Caddyfile so the `caddy` system user always
#      has read+traverse access to it - the data dir can be wherever
#      the operator wants (including a 0700 user home), since it isn't
#      on Caddy's read path.
#   2. Everything else reverse-proxies to the app. `reverse_proxy` auto-
#      upgrades to WebSocket for `/ws`, streams chunked bodies for
#      `/media/<hash>/<kind>`, and forwards `Range` headers - all of which
#      the audio/video transport needs.
http:// {
	handle /root.crt {
		root * /etc/caddy
		rewrite * /nightingale-root.crt
		file_server
	}
	handle {
		reverse_proxy 127.0.0.1:8080
	}
}

# ── HTTPS listener (opt-in) ─────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# `:443` matches every hostname/IP that arrives over TLS. `tls internal`
# tells Caddy to issue every certificate from its own local CA (no ACME,
# which would fail on a pure-LAN box without public DNS / port-80
# ingress). The `on_demand` subdirective mints a fresh cert for each SNI
# on first contact and caches it - so `<host>.local` and the bare LAN IP
# both work out of the box without templating per-host.
#
# This block stays here even when nobody installs the cert; the listener
# is harmless and gives users that want HTTPS a one-step path.
:443 {
	tls internal {
		on_demand
	}
	reverse_proxy 127.0.0.1:8080
}
